1
00:00:00,255 --> 00:00:02,930
An order issue arrives and the team
needs three things.

2
00:00:03,311 --> 00:00:06,488
Someone must explain the validator, find
the rule in our notes,

3
00:00:06,859 --> 00:00:07,831
and label the issue.

4
00:00:08,192 --> 00:00:11,482
Those tasks can share the same text, yet
the outputs are different.

5
00:00:11,903 --> 00:00:15,926
Start by naming the deliverable. Once
you know what must come back,

6
00:00:16,258 --> 00:00:19,077
you can pick a capability and a way to
check the result.

7
00:00:20,027 --> 00:00:24,535
Give Copilot the return line and ask it
to explain the result for zero and one.

8
00:00:24,966 --> 00:00:28,261
A language model can produce an
explanation in ordinary words.

9
00:00:28,662 --> 00:00:32,572
Check those words against the
expression. Both numbers satisfy this

10
00:00:32,572 --> 00:00:33,129
condition.

11
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:35,536
If the answer says zero is rejected,

12
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:39,343
you've found a specific sentence to
question and a one-line source to point

13
00:00:39,343 --> 00:00:39,629
to.

14
00:00:40,547 --> 00:00:43,366
Now imagine the rule lives in one of
three training notes.

15
00:00:43,787 --> 00:00:47,182
An embedding model turns the query and
the stored notes into vectors.

16
00:00:47,543 --> 00:00:50,780
The system compares those
representations and ranks possible

17
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:51,266
matches.

18
00:00:51,587 --> 00:00:54,322
A useful first result is the order
acceptance note.

19
00:00:54,873 --> 00:00:58,415
Open it and read the rule. Similarity
helps you find a source.

20
00:00:58,906 --> 00:01:01,101
The source itself tells you what the
rule says.

21
00:01:02,068 --> 00:01:05,652
For the issue label, our outputs are
defect, feature, or question.

22
00:01:05,932 --> 00:01:09,708
The ticket reports behavior that already
contradicts a documented rule.

23
00:01:09,988 --> 00:01:12,126
That gives us a reason to choose defect.

24
00:01:12,528 --> 00:01:14,946
A classifier can propose the label
directly,

25
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:18,540
and a language model can also choose
from a short allowed list.

26
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:22,579
Check which wording in the issue
supports the decision before applying

27
00:01:22,579 --> 00:01:22,745
it.

28
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:27,803
Think of the materials for a training
lesson. A screenshot can go into a

29
00:01:27,803 --> 00:01:29,931
vision-capable model for a description.

30
00:01:30,232 --> 00:01:33,338
A written brief can go into an image
model for an illustration.

31
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:36,418
Audio can go into a transcription system
for text.

32
00:01:36,779 --> 00:01:39,926
Check a screenshot description against
the visible controls.

33
00:01:40,397 --> 00:01:43,892
Check a transcript against an important
sentence in the recording.

34
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:48,858
Some tools combine these steps. A
question about zero quantities might

35
00:01:48,858 --> 00:01:50,869
first retrieve the most relevant note.

36
00:01:51,174 --> 00:01:53,865
The language model then uses that note
to draft an answer.

37
00:01:54,165 --> 00:01:57,468
When you see the final paragraph, trace
it back through the citation.

38
00:01:57,849 --> 00:02:02,384
If retrieval found an outdated rule, the
answer can sound convincing and still

39
00:02:02,435 --> 00:02:03,486
use the wrong evidence.

40
00:02:04,527 --> 00:02:08,058
Choose a task from the training
repository and finish this sentence.

41
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:11,004
“I need this input turned into this
output,

42
00:02:11,255 --> 00:02:12,969
and I will check it by doing this.”

43
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:17,718
For example, use a language model to
explain the validator and verify the

44
00:02:17,718 --> 00:02:19,799
explanation against the code and a test.

45
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:22,922
Once that check is clear, you're ready
to compare options.
